---
import { sequoiaSlides } from '../../../data/pitchDeckData';
---

      <article id="article-3" class="article-block">
        <!-- Article 3 Header -->
        <div class="article-header">
          <div class="article-category-badge">
            <span class="badge-dot"></span>
            <span>ARTICLE 03 &bull; SILICON VALLEY GOLD STANDARD &bull; SEQUOIA CAPITAL</span>
          </div>

          <h2 class="article-title">
            The Sequoia Capital Pitch Deck Framework (10 Slides)
          </h2>

          <p class="article-lead">
            Sequoia Capital's pitch deck framework is one of the most revered in Silicon Valley, famously utilized by companies like Airbnb in their earliest stages. The core philosophy behind this framework is not just about the slides themselves, but about testing the founder's clarity of thought and the sheer scope of their ambition. Sequoia specifically looks for founders who are determined to build something that conventional wisdom considers impossible.
          </p>

          <div class="article-meta-bar">
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Reading time:</strong> 9 min read</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Firm:</strong> Sequoia Capital</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Format:</strong> 10-Slide Master Blueprint</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Quick Navigation Bar for Article 3 -->
        <nav class="toc-nav" aria-label="Slide quick links">
          <span class="toc-label">Jump to slide:</span>
          <div class="toc-pills">
            {sequoiaSlides.map((slide) => (
              <a href={`#seq-slide-${parseInt(slide.num)}`} class="toc-pill">
                {slide.num}. {slide.title.split(' ')[0]}
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </nav>

        <!-- Sequoia 10-Slide Overview Cards Grid -->
        <section class="summary-cards-section">
          <div class="summary-cards-grid">
            {sequoiaSlides.map((slide) => (
              <a href={`#seq-slide-${parseInt(slide.num)}`} class="summary-card">
                <div class="card-num-row">
                  <span class="slide-badge">SLIDE {slide.num}</span>
                  <span class="slide-question">{slide.headline}</span>
                </div>
                <h3 class="card-slide-title">{slide.title}</h3>
                <p class="card-slide-desc">{slide.desc}</p>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="section-divider" />

        <!-- Sequoia Slide Deep Dives (1 to 10) -->
        <div class="key-points-list">
          
          <!-- Slide 1: Company Purpose -->
          <div id="seq-slide-1" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">01</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 1</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">1. Company Purpose</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              You must define your company's reason for existing in a <strong>single, declarative sentence</strong>. This is often harder than it looks because founders tend to list product features instead of communicating a durable, overarching mission. It needs to be a specific and aspirational statement rather than just a generic tagline.
            </p>
            <div class="callout-tip">
              <strong>Sequoia Test:</strong> Does your purpose statement describe the timeless mission of your enterprise, or does it merely describe your initial v1.0 software feature?
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 2: Problem -->
          <div id="seq-slide-2" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">02</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 2</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">2. Problem</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              This slide must clearly describe the pain of your customer, how they are addressing it today, and the critical shortcomings of those current workarounds. You must prove the pain is real and quantified. For enterprise startups, this means showing a detailed understanding of the customer's financial or operational pain, while consumer startups must highlight deep user needs.
            </p>
            <ul class="framework-checklist">
              <li>Quantify the financial or operational drag experienced by the customer.</li>
              <li>Outline the exact patchwork solutions and workarounds used today.</li>
              <li>Expose why current alternatives are fundamentally unsustainable.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 3: Solution -->
          <div id="seq-slide-3" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">03</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 3</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">3. Solution</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Here, you explain your <strong>"eureka moment"</strong> and demonstrate why your value proposition is unique, compelling, and built to endure. If possible, use a demo, screenshots, or a user workflow to bring the solution to life, as a visual demonstration is highly effective at this stage. The solution must directly and logically map back to the problem you just established.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 4: Why Now? -->
          <div id="seq-slide-4" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">04</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 4</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">4. Why Now?</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Sequoia considers this a critical test: <strong>since nature hates a vacuum, you must explain why your solution hasn't been built before</strong>. You need to identify a specific, recent inflection point—such as a technological breakthrough, a behavioral shift, or a regulatory change—that suddenly opened the window to create this new company.
            </p>
            <div class="callout-conclusion">
              <strong>The Inflection Window:</strong> What shifted in the macro environment in the last 12-24 months that makes your company possible today, whereas 5 years ago it would have failed?
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 5: Market Potential -->
          <div id="seq-slide-5" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">05</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 5</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">5. Market Potential</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Identify your target customer and the size of the market. Rather than throwing out massive, top-down market study numbers without detail, investors want to see a <strong>bottom-up calculation</strong>: how many actual users or customers exist, how that number grows, and how much each customer is worth. Sequoia also notes that the best companies often invent entirely new markets.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 6: Competition / Alternatives -->
          <div id="seq-slide-6" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">06</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 6</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">6. Competition / Alternatives</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Identify both your direct and indirect competitors. It is much better to proactively identify all competitors than to let investors discover them later; this allows you to clearly explain your specific differentiation and how you plan to win.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 7: Business Model -->
          <div id="seq-slide-7" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">07</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 7</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">7. Business Model</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              This slide explains how you intend to thrive economically. You should detail your revenue model, pricing strategy, average account size, and your sales and distribution plan.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 8: Team -->
          <div id="seq-slide-8" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">08</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 8</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">8. Team</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Tell the story of your founders and key management. Highlight the specific talents, past successes, or unique experiences that make this specific team perfectly suited to build this exact business.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 9: Financials -->
          <div id="seq-slide-9" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">09</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 9</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">9. Financials</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Share your financial data, which typically includes your P&amp;L, balance sheet, cash flow, and cap table. However, you should keep the narrative simple by showing a <strong>timeline of exactly how you plan to spend the raised capital (e.g., headcount) to hit specific developmental milestones</strong>.
            </p>
            <div class="callout-warning">
              <strong>Sequoia Financial Rule:</strong> Never present financial projections as a passive burn rate. Anchor every dollar of headcount and expenditure to a concrete product or revenue milestone.
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 10: Vision -->
          <div id="seq-slide-10" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">10</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">SEQUOIA SLIDE 10</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">10. Vision</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Conclude by zooming out. <strong>Explain what the company will look like in five years if everything goes according to plan.</strong> This is your final chance to reinforce the massive scope of your ambition.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </article>
